      \field{abstract}{In this thesis, I present a collection of full-waveform seismology studies with the goal to investigate subsurface structure and seismic sources. These studies rely on data from seismometers and novel fibre-optic sensing techniques. I introduce a new global tomographic model based on seismometer data using full-waveform inversion (FWI). The Collaborative Seismic Earth Model: Generation 2 (CSEM2) is a multi-scale model that combines 22 regional and global tomographic models into one coherent global framework, consistently incorporating prior knowledge. To mitigate artefacts at submodel boundaries and further image mantle structure using the refined surface models, we perform a global inversion for a minimum period of 50 s, using the assembled submodels as the starting point. We demonstrate that CSEM2 accurately captures wave propagation at the global scale for the inverted target period while retaining the capability to explain shorter periods within the submodels. Additionally, a side project in the context of supervising a Master student leads to the development of a new tomographic model for Europe and Western Asia, using CSEM2 as the starting model. The main topic of this thesis is the development of a theoretical framework for performing source and structural inversions using full waveforms measured with fibre-optic data. This effectively transfers knowledge from global-scale FWI to this new type of data. Fibre-optic sensing, particularly distributed acoustic sensing (DAS), has emerged over the past decade as a seismic interrogation method applicable in various environments, including offshore, boreholes, landslides, glaciers, volcanoes, and urban areas. Its appeal lies in its flexibility and relative ease of deployment. However, interpreting this data to infer seismic source properties and subsurface structure is complicated by variability in fibre orientation, gauge length effects, and the measurement unit of strain. All these challenges are addressed in the FWI method presented in this thesis. The method is demonstrated through inversion examples with artificial data that showcase the powerful imaging capabilities of DAS. The associated code is publicly available as the software package Pyber In several collaborative side projects, briefly outlined here, Pyber is already being applied to inversions on various DAS datasets to investigate volcanic sources, icequakes, hydraulic stimulations, and the structure of an active landslide. While FWI is an elegant method for maximising the used data to invert for structures, it suffers from the lack of a feasible and conclusive uncertainty quantification. Due to computational constraints, comprehensive exploration of the null space and model resolution is not feasible even for relatively small problems, and especially not for most relevant seismic inverse problems. This thesis presents a more efficient approach to quantify point-spread functions using source encoding. We demonstrate, using a 2-D ultrasound imaging example of a mouse, that this method is viable and can significantly reduce computation time for point-wise model resolution assessment. Within another project, we perform a hypothesis test on an unexpected, fast, and prominent anomaly found in the global tomographic model REVEAL in the mid-mantle beneath the western Pacific, a region that is hardly covered by seismic stations. This anomaly is not imaged in travel-time tomographies. The test shows that the full-waveform data is indeed sensitive to this region, which leads us to challenge the common assumption in plate tectonic reconstructions that all fast anomalies represent subducting plates. We argue for a more nuanced interpretation as increasingly high-resolution tomographic models of Earth's interior become available. While the presented methods do not solve the issues of uncertainty quantification in FWI, they represent a step towards overcoming this challenge. Moreover, this thesis presents a novel seismic measurement technique that uses active phase-noise cancellation (PNC) of the laser signal transmitted through the entire length of the fibre, a form of integrated fibre-optic sensing (IFOS). While the dense channel sampling along the fibre as seen in DAS is lost, because the entire fibre length is treated as a single channel, this technique significantly extends the maximum interrogation length from tens of kilometers to potentially over a thousand. In close collaboration with the Swiss Metrological Service, we present the successful numerical reconstruction of an M3.9 earthquake near Mulhouse, France, measured on a 123 km fibre-optic link between two laboratories in Basel and Bern with PNC. Additional contributions to side projects using the same PNC acquisition technique include source inversion, ambient noise analysis, and earthquake measurements in Switzerland and globally. We also explore the potential for source and structural inversions with full-waveforms on artificial IFOS data. This work lays the foundation for future FWI applications as more IFOS data sets become available.}
